  "executive_summary": "The Docker environment hosting the ICS lab presents critical security risks due to containers running as root, excessive capabilities, and unpatched vulnerabilities in critical components. The simulation container's Docker socket mount (E22) allows full Docker API access, enabling container escapes and arbitrary container creation. The router container (E2) has CAP_NET_ADMIN and CAP_NET_RAW capabilities while exposing GRE port 51820/udp, creating a high-risk attack vector for network manipulation. Both EWS (E7) and HMI (E10) containers use default credentials (VNC_PASSWORD=changeme, MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=root), and the PLC (E19) container has unpatched Perl and Linux kernel vulnerabilities. These weaknesses could allow attackers to disrupt ICS operations, manipulate control systems, or escalate privileges to the host. Network segmentation is weak: the simulation container (E22) and router (E2) bridge admin and ICS networks, while the kali (E16) and caldera (E13) red team containers are on the admin network with no isolation. Image scanning (E5, E8, E11, E14, E17, E20, E23) revealed 36 critical vulnerabilities across containers, with the simulation container's GnuTLS (CVE-2026-33845) and OpenSSL (CVE-2026-31789) vulnerabilities posing direct risks to secure communications. No containers have resource limits configured, increasing denial-of-service risks for critical ICS components. The assessment was limited to running containers and could not verify network driver configurations or host-level firewall rules.",
